      drivers: "SNB policy rate 0.00% TE-verified and unchanged since mid-2025; inflation TE-verified at 1.0% y/y in September from 0.8% with core 0.5% and m/m 0.0%, and the SNB's own September forecast of a further rise in Q4 before easing through 2027, averaging 0.7% in 2026 and 0.8% in 2027 and 2028; unemployment 3.0%, the lowest of the eight; Q2 GDP plus 2.3% y/y, the strongest here, with the SNB projecting 1.5-2% for 2026; Q2 employment plus 0.5%; ten-year 0.5570, the lowest of the eight by a factor of five; centralbank.watch prices the December 10 assessment at a 21.9% hike against 78.1% hold; USD/CHF 0.82985 at minus 0.61% on the day, plus 0.49% on the month and plus 4.66% year to date, so the dollar still holds a substantial annual gain on the franc; EUR/CHF 0.93072, back below the 0.93229 of yesterday; AUD/CHF minus 0.87% and CAD/CHF minus 0.41%, the two largest franc gains, against the two currencies whose central banks just got less hawkish.",
      forward: "🦅 Hawkish-leaning for the first time in this file's run, and the upgrade is regime-contingent rather than structural - which is the honest way to hold it. The structural problem has not changed: the franc pays 0.00% while every other currency here pays something, and in a world where the dominant risk-off driver is an oil shock the franc loses, because an oil shock is an inflation shock that other central banks can respond to and the SNB cannot. This file tested that proposition five times against escalating Middle East headlines and the franc failed every time. Wednesday supplied the first shock of a different type. A credit event centred on debt-funded AI buildouts is a demand shock, and in a demand shock the zero-yielding currency with the cleanest balance sheet is the asset of choice - which is why the franc took the haven flow that the yen did not and the dollar could not. The forward case therefore rests on a conditional that should be stated rather than buried: this is a plus 1 for as long as the marginal risk-off impulse is credit rather than crude. The second support is new and is not a judgement call - centralbank.watch is pricing December 10 at a 21.9% hike, which for a bank at zero is a real number, and it sits on a rising inflation path the SNB itself has forecast to rise further this quarter. The institutional ambiguity is the thing to watch and it works both ways: TE's page carries the SNB saying it remains willing to intervene in FX as necessary, while Bloomberg read the same September 24 meeting as the Bank dialling that threat down in favour of vigilance on inflation. If the former is operative, a franc rally gets fought; if the latter, it runs. Schlegel spoke at 04:30 UTC this morning and nothing is indexed - not verifiable here - with the October 22 minutes the next scheduled read. Invalidated back to 0 by a return to an oil-led risk regime with the franc again failing to bid, by Schlegel reinstating intervention language, or by the December 10 hike probability falling back toward single digits; confirmed toward plus 2 if the AI-credit repricing extends and EUR/CHF breaks the September range low."
