      forward: "⚖️ Balanced into October 30, and the single most informative thing about the yen today is a comparison rather than a number. Wednesday delivered the first genuine credit scare of this cycle, the kind of event a haven currency exists for, and the yen gained 0.24% against the dollar while losing ground to the franc, the euro, sterling and the kiwi. The franc took the flow; the yen did not. That is a currency functioning as a funding vehicle rather than a haven, and at a 157-158 handle with a 218bp differential that is exactly what it should be expected to do. The mechanism stated here for weeks is that the yen does not need the BoJ to hike, it needs the gap to compress, and the gap did compress for the first time in this run - by 2.3bp, which is a direction worth recording and not a reason to change a score. The domestic data is not helping the hawkish case in the near term: core CPI fell to 1.7% from 1.8%, wage growth slowed to 3.8% from 4.3%, and October 29-30 is priced at 86.9% hold with 0% for a hike. Against that, the institution is clearly in motion - a 7-2 September hike delivered on the shortest interval since 1990, most policymakers signalling more, and Momma's framing that an October move is a 20-30% possibility while delay past January is extremely low. So the BoJ is going; the market says December. The thing that is larger today than it was yesterday is the tail, and it belongs on every long USD/JPY ticket here rather than on the yen's score. A yen-funded carry unwind is the canonical consequence of a credit event, and Wednesday was the first one. It did not force an unwind - USD/JPY is 157.947, barely moved - but the configuration now exists, and the asymmetry is poor: the carry pays slowly and the unwind pays all at once. Raised to plus 1 by an October 30 Outlook Report that lifts the inflation forecast while the Fed's December hike is delivered as the last one, or by the ten-year gap compressing through 200bp; cut to minus 1 by a Warsh-driven repricing of the Fed above December with the BoJ still on hold into 2027."
